 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "HSAB.11 Protecting oneself from danger and accidents: notices that running on the road is dangerous; stops at the zebra crossing, looks at the light, listens; when it turns green, looks left, right and left again and crosses holding a grown-up's hand (avoids dangerous behaviour and situations).",
   "SBAB7 Spatial Thinking: uses position words such as across, beside, left and right on the road and the zebra crossing in the story.",
   "OB6 Citizenship Literacy: recognises shared rules that everyone follows, such as traffic lights and zebra crossings; says that following the rules protects everyone.",
   "SDB3.3 Responsible Decision-Making and D16 Responsibility: chooses the safe option between the urge to run and waiting; D12 Patience: uses coping strategies such as counting, stroking and playing while waiting."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show the traffic light on page 2: “Where have you seen this? What colours does it have?”",
   "Ask: “What do we do when we cross the road?” and listen to the answers without judging.",
   "You can link it to Traffic and First Aid Week (the first week of May)."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 2, stop at Can's words “Let's run!”: “Why did his mum say, ‘Let's look at the light first’?”",
   "On page 3, look at Can's body: his feet are going tap, tap on the ground. “Does waiting feel long to you too?”",
   "On page 4, say Can's “Stop, look, listen!” game out loud together with the children and do it with movements.",
   "On page 5, do the look left, right, left again movement together. It's fine if left and right get mixed up; show it with your head together, saying ‘first one way, then the other way, then the first way again’.",
   "On page 6, let the child do the hand-in-hand crossing, then ask: “Who is Cinnamon walking next to?” If we have a dog, the lead is always in our hand on the road."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Ask: “What did Can do while he waited? What would you have done?”",
   "Ask: “Why did the cars stop when the green man lit up? What happens when everyone follows the rule?”",
   "Play a “Stop or cross?” game with red man and green man cards.",
   "Sing Can's song together: “Red light, we stop! Green light, we walk!”"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Classroom Street: Stop, Look, Listen, Cross",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "White tape or chalk (zebra crossing stripes), a red and a green pedestrian card, toy cars or steering wheel cards",
   "adim": [
    "Make a zebra crossing on the floor with tape; the two edges are the pavements.",
    "One child is the traffic light and holds up the red man or green man card; a few children are “cars” and drive slowly along the road.",
    "The pedestrians say “Stop, look, listen!” on the pavement; they wait at red, look left and right, and when it turns green they cross holding a friend's hand.",
    "Swap roles; at the end, talk about “What did you do while you waited, and how did you feel?”"
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "What did Can look at before he crossed the road?",
   "What does the red man mean, and what does the green man mean?",
   "What can you do when waiting feels too long?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Neighbourhood Light Hunt",
   "d": "While walking together, find the zebra crossings and traffic lights. At every crossing, say “Stop, look, listen!” with your child, and cross hand in hand when it turns green. While you wait, you can count the cars or sing Can's song."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Green Light, Let's Walk.” Can wanted to run to the park across the road, but he stopped at the zebra crossing with his mum, looked at the light and turned waiting into a game he made up himself. The children recognised the red man and the green man on the traffic light and learnt the order “Stop, look, listen!” Setting an example on the road is the strongest teacher: always cross at the zebra crossing, on green, looking left-right-left and holding your child's hand; if you have a dog, keep the lead in your hand at all times."
 },
